WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.695
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.695 --> 00:00:09.575
When they invited me I just though about
like, talk about what.

00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:11.628
I didn't wanna give another talk about

00:00:11.628 --> 00:00:14.243
globalization, or the middle east I
thought

00:00:14.243 --> 00:00:18.310
I'd actually talk to you today about what
I do which is writing a column.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:24.130
And how to write a column, what
constitutes a column and what I've

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:28.810
learned in the process of writing a column
for the last 20 years.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.630
So that's what my talk is gonna be about
today.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.450
Be three parts, let's say the first part

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.350
will be about where my own opinions came
from.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:40.390
What, what are the roots of, of my outlook
on the world.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:44.820
The second part will be about the nine
different kinds of columns.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.920
And for there are many more, but to me
there are at least nine.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.980
And the last part'll be about what I've
learned.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.190
I'm actually thinking about turning this
in to a book.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.920
And it all kind of started, the idea.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.010
Really just about three months ago I was
in Saudi Arabia.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:04.050
And I was meeting with a group from the
Acumen Fund.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.990
And and entrepreneurial philanthropy.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.700
The Saudi chapter.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.300
Who knew that there was a Saudi chapter?

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:10.810
But there is.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:14.440
And a very impressive group of young Saudi
men and women were

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.610
meeting at the home of one of the women in
the group.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.330
And after dinner, we were sitting around.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.000
We were sitting around with these young
Saudis and they

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.190
were asking me about how do you write a
column?

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:30.370
And I said once, well, she taught a course
once for my, other

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:34.850
daughter, Williams College during their
winter term on how to write a column.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.800
And I said there were really just nine
kinds of columns.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.370
And if you write one of these nine, you've
got a column.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:43.280
And I started ticking them off as we were
sitting around the table.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.120
And I noticed right in the middle of it.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.880
They all took out their cell phones, and
were

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.745
writing down with their thumbs everything
I was saying.

00:01:51.745 --> 00:01:54.770
[INAUDIBLE] And when, when one missed one,
was, I missed number four.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:55.320
Did you get four?

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.140
What was seven?

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:56.740
You know?

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:01.930
And I was sort of watching this process,
and thinking about it afterwards.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.920
And it really struck me, that we are in an

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:11.560
age now where everyone wants to, and can,
really opine.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:12.050
And be a columnist.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.150
You know, I always tell people, when I
started the New York

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:21.305
Times, as a columnist, in January 1995, I
actually inherited James Reston's office.

00:02:21.305 --> 00:02:24.040
[INAUDIBLE] in the Washington Bureau of
the New York Times.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:27.660
What a great thrill and honor to have the
office that was used

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:31.190
by this great columnist and editor of the
times in the 60s and 70s.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.710
And I suspect when Mister Reston came to
the office back in the 60s and 70s.

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.160
He would start every day by saying, I
wonder

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.870
what my seven competitors are gonna write
about today?

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.940
And he personally knew all seven of his
competitors.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:45.920
I can name them.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.500
Walter Lippmann, Mary [UNKNOWN],
[INAUDIBLE] Alsop, Joseph.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.080
Craft, Tony Lewis, I, I know them.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.170
I now do the same thing, I come to the
office every day and I

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:58.335
say to myself, I wonder what my 70 million
competitors are going to write today.

00:02:58.335 --> 00:03:00.370
[LAUGH]

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:03.710
I have 70 million competitors, cuz we do
live in a world where.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:08.230
Anyone who has access to a blog is

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.970
now a potential columnist, everyone who
has access

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:13.460
twitter is a reporter, anyone with access
to

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.390
YouTube is a film maker, or a paparazzi.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:24.950
And so so many people now are engaged in,
or pining about the world.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:30.070
In their own ways and I think it's
actually very exciting and it's very rich

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:35.120
and everyone is doing it in their own way
and what

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.160
I want to talk to you about for the next
40 minutes or

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.170
so and then we'll have some Q and A, is
what is my way.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.390
I don't know that it's the best way.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:50.520
It certainly isn't the only way, but I can
tell you it's my way.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:56.350
Now my way starts and I think every
columnist has to

00:03:56.350 --> 00:04:02.109
start with with a point of view with a
take on the world.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.220
No one's interested, in your disconnected,
thoughts.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:12.610
Everyone has a take on the world, and if I
do write this, up in a book, this chapter

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:18.170
will be called, always looking for
Minnesota.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:22.690
You won't understand my way, without
understanding where I come from.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:25.160
And how my world view was shaped.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.500
So let's go back to the beginning.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.930
When I first fell in love with journalism.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:35.050
It all starts back in Minnesota, with my
own version of the Jersey Boys.

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:39.520
The musical about the 1960s singing group,
the Four Seasons.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:40.390
Led by Frankie Valli.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.640
It's one of my favorite musicals, and I
particularly like the

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:44.990
opening monologue when the group's

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:48.430
founder, Tommy Devito, locates their
beginning.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:50.940
Devito comes onstage, following a French
rendition of

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.590
the Four Season's Classic, Oh What a
Night.

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:56.390
And, he begins by saying, that's our song,
Oh

00:04:56.390 --> 00:05:00.746
What a Night, [FOREIGN] French, number 1
in Paris.

00:05:00.746 --> 00:05:01.828
2000.

00:05:01.828 --> 00:05:04.000
How'd that happen?

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.005
You ask four guys, you get four different

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:09.040
versions, but this is where all of them
start.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.468
Bellville, New Jersey, 1000 years ago.

00:05:11.468 --> 00:05:16.210
Eisenhower, Rocky Marciano, and a few guys
under

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:19.800
a street lamp singing somebody else's
latest hit.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.270
Every time I listen to that riff it
transports me back to my own roots.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:27.010
To this day, I pinch myself that I've been
the

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.810
foreign affairs columnist for The New York
Times for 20 years.

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:30.870
How did that happen?

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:36.080
You ask my family and friends, you get 40
different versions.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.280
But this is where all of them start.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.460
Minnesota.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:40.506
A thousand years ago.

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:45.930
Hubert Humphrey, 10,000 lakes, the
Minnesota Vikings, and a

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:50.930
few guys and girls, including me, growing
up in a tiny, one high school suburb

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:55.750
outside of Minneapolis called St. Louis
Park.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:58.558
Something was in the water there in the
1960's.

00:05:58.558 --> 00:06:01.700
During those post-war years, this tiny

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.430
community, St. Louis Park, ten square
miles,.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.030
Was the birthplace or childhood home of
the

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:10.190
movie directors, the Coen Brothers, Joel
and Ethan Coen.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.330
Political scientist Norman Ornstein.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.320
Senator and former comedian, Al Franken.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.520
Classical guitarist Sharon Isbon.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.760
Chicago Bears football coach Mark Tristan.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.550
Film director Joe Nussbaum.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:24.050
Harvard University political philosopher
Michael Nussbaum.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:24.950
And yours truly.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:30.730
We all grew up in about six block radius
of one another during the 60s.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:35.260
The Coen brothers, you may know, based
their 2009 movie A Serious Man

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.170
on St. Louis Park circa 1967 and

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:40.890
among other things memorialized our Hebrew
school.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:43.050
When they were young, the Coen brothers
often hung out at

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.790
Mike Zoss Drugs on Minnetonka Boulevard, a
few miles from my house.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:49.140
And if you look closely in their classic
film No

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:52.580
Country For Old Men, the pharmacy just
across the Mexican

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:56.150
border that the lead character Chigurh,
played by Javier Bardem,

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.040
enters to get medicine after he blows up a
parked car.

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:03.230
Is called Mike's [INAUDIBLE] Pharmacy.

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:07.130
Vice President Mondale once asked me to
speak for

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:10.230
his Minneapolis law firm and in
preparation for introducing me,

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:13.380
interviewed the Cohan brothers, Frank and
Ornstein, on what they

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:16.050
thought was going on in Saint Louis Park
back then.

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:18.980
Frank had said, when people hear that the
five of

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:21.520
us all grew up in the same suburb, they're
astonished.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:23.860
What's in the water, they sometimes joked.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:24.730
But it's not a joke.

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:27.960
During our childhood, at St. Louis park,
it was home to a

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:33.340
large creosote plant, which leached tons
of toxic materials into the groundwater.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:37.220
Studies have shown that ingesting large
quantities of creosote can lead

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:38.520
two things increased levels of

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.390
intellectual creativity and or prostate
problems.

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:46.200
The Cohen brothers compare Saint Louis
park to the small

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:51.080
region in Hungary that had produced
numerous nuclear physicists and draculas.

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.880
My English teacher, Miriam Kagel, who
later taught journalism at

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:56.810
Park High told the Jewish Daily Forward,
it was partly rooted

00:07:56.810 --> 00:07:58.840
in the migration of Jews to St. Louis Park
from the

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:02.680
inner city of Minneapolis, the northside,
in the 40s and 50s.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:06.330
It was the time of great political ferment
had reached the Midwest and

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:07.750
people were just full of ideas and

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:11.110
protests and opinions and speaking their
mind.

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:13.260
I think there is something to that.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:17.410
In my case, my parents subscribed to Time,
and Life magazines, and

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.030
to both the morning and afternoon

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:22.470
newspapers, the Minneapolis Tribune and
Minneapolis Star.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:26.390
That is where I trace my love of
journalism, and opinion writing.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:28.460
I don't know why, but from the moment I
started

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:29.820
reading newspapers, probably from in

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.840
junior highschool, I enjoyed reading
columnists.

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:34.420
I can still see myself coming home from

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:37.290
school, picking up the Minneapolis Star
and spreading

00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:39.380
it out on the livingroom floor and reading

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:43.190
Peter Listagor's syndicated column from
the Chicago Tribune.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:45.270
I devoured Time Magazine as soon as it
arrived,

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:48.380
and I got my first experience in opinion
writing.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:51.430
By writing a letter to the editor of Time
magazine to

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:56.130
protest a swipe that Time magazine took at
my favorite baseball

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:59.870
player in those days, the Minnesota Twins
talented utility player Cesar

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:04.270
Tovar, who once played all 9 positions
during a single game.

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.730
Time criticized the fact that Tovar got
one vote for

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:10.730
the American Leagues most valuable player
award in the 1967 season.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:14.590
Triple Crown winner Carl Yastrzemski
resol, received all the other votes.

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:17.500
I rose to Tovar's defense as a worthy MVP

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:21.340
candidate with my outraged letter to the
editor of Time.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:23.170
14 years old, at the time, I did not get
my

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.780
letter published, but I did get a letter
back from Time.

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:27.090
That I saved for many years.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:32.750
It was on crisp white Time stationery,
addressed to Mr. Friedman.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:34.670
And I was very impressed.

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:37.850
Somewhere there an opinion columnist was
being conceived.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.040
My first exposure, though, to the
substance

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:44.240
of column writing, that is to politics

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:48.250
and business, came from being a caddy for
my father and some of his pals.

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:51.120
At the local golf club called Brookview.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.100
There was a little bit of a Jewish Mafia

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.080
in Minneapolis back in the late 1940s and
50s.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:59.220
And as a very young caddy, I remember
asking my father

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:04.070
about someone he knew who was convicted
and sentenced to jail time.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:06.600
I asked what he had done, and my dad
gently explained to

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.470
me that he was, quote, shopping in a store
before it was open.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:15.350
One of the great metaphors of all time and

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.970
maybe that is where my love of metaphors
came from.

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.620
I learned lots of worldly things as a
caddy

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:24.820
about whose business was doing well, who
was going bankrupt.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:25.610
In fact.

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:29.380
First summer I learned what bankruptcy is
was on the,on the golf course.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:32.880
I actually got my first sex education
course caddying also.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:38.410
I caddied in 1970 U.S. Open for ChiChi
Rodriguez, a famous Puerto Rican golfer.

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:41.080
And one day ChiChi and I were standing on
the practice tee and ChiChi

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:42.440
was hitting shots with his hands while

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:44.790
providing a running commentary with his
mouth.

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:47.080
There was a small crowd watching from
behind.

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:48.750
You know, I used to be a teacher, Chi

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:51.410
Chi told the fans, as he walked [UNKNOWN]
the balls.

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:52.260
But I gave that up.

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:52.740
You know why?

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:53.260
You know why?

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:54.770
I had this woman, she was a slicer.

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:55.760
I turned her into a hooker.

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:57.840
And after that, I gave up teaching.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:10:59.130
I was 17 at the time.

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:01.560
I confess, I did not know what a hooker
was.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.420
And all the adults around me were
laughing.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:06.380
My father explained it to me.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:08.640
My first sex education class.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.200
But most of all what I took away from
Minnesota

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:17.990
in the fifties and sixties that has
influenced me my whole

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:21.910
life was a strong sense of belonging to a
community

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:25.830
and a certain optimism about how a
community can come together.

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:30.170
Through politics and philanthropy to make
people's lives better.

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:33.890
To put it simply, I learned from Minnesota
that politics can work.

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:36.170
Indeed in 1971, the year I graduated high

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:39.200
school, Time Magazine wrote a cover story
with our

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:41.145
governor Wendell Anderson on the cover
holding up

00:11:41.145 --> 00:11:44.970
wall-eye, under the headline Minnesota,
the state that works.

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:48.070
When your senator's growing up are called

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:51.930
Eugene McCarthy, Walter Mondale, and
Hubert Humphry.

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:55.070
When the big companies around you are
called 3M, Dayton Hudson,

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:58.810
Target and UUU control data and they
believe it is their obligation

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:02.520
to build the symphony hall in the Tyrone
Guthrie theater, that corporate

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.840
social responsibility was something they
practiced

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:07.020
long before it was ever invented.

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:09.070
When your Congressmen in your

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:12.551
overwhelmingly Democratic district are two
liberal

00:12:12.551 --> 00:12:18.660
Republicans and when your Congressman
today is the only African-American Muslim

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:22.868
in the US Congress, Keith Ellison, who is
from St. Louis

00:12:22.868 --> 00:12:27.590
Park, you grow up believing that politics
can actually bring people together.

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:29.960
Bridge differences, and solve problems.

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:34.600
And that is the gift I took from my 18
years growing up in Minnesota.

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:35.960
The belief in the possibility.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:39.190
Not the certainty, but the possibility,
that people will do the

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:42.640
right thing, and the outcome will be win
win for everyone.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:45.210
So if there is a consistent theme to my
column, no matter

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:49.290
what the issue or problem, is to show
people the avenues and possibilities.

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:52.220
For compromise and win-win solutions.

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:56.510
I know that things can turn out well
because I saw it happen in my lifetime.

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:59.990
So that's why the title to, you know, this
chapter

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:06.000
and maybe to my whole career is always
looking for Minnesota.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.610
Always looking for the middle ground.

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.400
Always hoping to find the better angels

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:12.110
from Beirut to Jerusalem to Washington,
DC.

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:14.330
But also not being surprised when I don't.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:16.140
I know the world is not Minnesota.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:18.400
You cannot live for a decade in Beirut and
Jerusalem and

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:22.110
cover wars and massacres and believe that
the world is Minnesota.

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:25.580
I recognize that pessimists are usually
right and I recognize

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:28.270
that even more so after twenty years of
writing a column.

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:31.780
But I also recognize that all the great
change in history was done by optimists.

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:35.720
Those who saw the possibility for building
win-win outcomes.

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:37.530
And so I make no apologies for trying to

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:41.520
buttress those people, and shine a light
on their efforts.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.600
The world has enough pessimists.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:47.410
I have the optimism corner, all to myself.

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:51.180
And growing up in Minnesota in the sixties
and seventies is where that came from.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:55.170
My actual writing career started in two
stages.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:59.200
The first was in high school, at Saint
Louis Park High in 1969.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:01.290
When I took journalism as a sophomore from

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:04.900
our legendary high school journalism
teacher, Hattie Steinberg.

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:07.930
People often speak about the teacher who
changed their lives.

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:09.900
Hattie changed mine.

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:13.410
I took her Introduction to Journalism
course in tenth grade in 1967 in

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:20.240
room 313 and I have never needed or taken
another course in journalism since.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:21.820
It was not that I was that good.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:24.040
It was that she was that good.

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:25.920
Hattie was a woman who believed that the
secret

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:28.880
for success in life was getting the
fundamentals right.

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:32.370
And boy, she pounded the fundamentals of
journalism into her students.

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:36.350
Not simply how to write a lead or accurate
desc, transcribe a quote, but

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:38.090
more important how to comport yourself in

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:41.730
a professional way and always do quality
work.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:43.340
To this day, when I forget to wear a

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:46.220
tie on assignment, I think of Hattie
scolding me.

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:49.920
I once interviewed an ad exec for a high
school paper who used a four letter word.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:50.842
We debated whether to run it.

00:14:50.842 --> 00:14:54.867
Had he ruled yes, that ad man almost lost
his job when it appeared.

00:14:54.867 --> 00:14:58.020
She wanted to teach us about consequences.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.260
Hatty was the toughest teacher I ever had.

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:02.620
After you took her journalism course in
tenth grade.

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:05.880
You tried out for the paper the echo which
he supervised.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:07.903
Competition was fierce in 11th grade I

00:15:07.903 --> 00:15:10.600
didn't quite come up to her writing
standards.

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:15.860
So she made me the business manager
selling pizza adds to local pizza parlors.

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:17.652
That year though she let me cover one

00:15:17.652 --> 00:15:20.222
story, it was about an Israeli general
who'd been

00:15:20.222 --> 00:15:21.773
a hero in the 6th day war who was

00:15:21.773 --> 00:15:25.470
visiting, giving a lecture at the
University of Minnesota.

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:29.020
I covered his lecture and interviewed him
briefly afterwards, his name

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:32.960
was Ariel Sharon, it was the first story I
ever got published.

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:35.229
We would meet each other many times after
that.

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:37.686
Those of us on the paper in the

00:15:37.686 --> 00:15:41.280
yearbook that she supervised, lived in
Hatty's classroom.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:43.999
We hang out there before and after school,
now you

00:15:43.999 --> 00:15:47.044
have to understand Hatty was a single
woman nearing 60

00:15:47.044 --> 00:15:49.372
at the time, and she was the 19, and this

00:15:49.372 --> 00:15:52.930
was the 1960s the was the polar opposite
of cool.

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:54.960
But we hung around her classroom like it

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:58.270
was the malt shop, and she was Wolfman
Jack.

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:01.900
None of us could articulated it then, but
it was because we

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:05.550
enjoyed being harangued by her,
disciplined by her, and taught by her.

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:08.210
She was a woman of clarity, in an age of
uncertainty.

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:11.398
We remained friends for 30 years, and she
followed

00:16:11.398 --> 00:16:14.300
and bragged about and critiqued every
twist in my career.

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:15.950
After she died, her friend sent me a pile

00:16:15.950 --> 00:16:18.320
of my stories that she had saved over the
years.

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:20.760
Indeed her students were her family, only
closer.

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:23.610
Judy Harrington, one of Hatty's former
student remarked, about

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:25.750
other friends who were on Hatty's
newspaper and yearbook.

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:29.400
We all graduated 41 years ago, yet nearly
each day in our lives,

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:31.670
something comes up, some mental image,
some

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:34.440
admonition that makes us think of Hatty.

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:37.374
Judy also told the story of one of Hatty's
last birthday parties when

00:16:37.374 --> 00:16:40.470
a man said he had to leave early to take
his daughter somewhere.

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:43.010
Sit down, said Hatty, you're not leaving
yet.

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:45.090
She can just be a little late.

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:46.640
That was my teacher.

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:49.120
I sit up straight just thinking about her.

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:55.560
Basically, after high school I went to did
my undergraduate at Brandeis, but I.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:59.179
Did semesters abroad at the Hebrew
University in

00:16:59.179 --> 00:17:01.657
Jerusalem and the American university in
Cairo I

00:17:01.657 --> 00:17:03.954
got a marshal scholarship to go to Oxford

00:17:03.954 --> 00:17:06.130
to study Arabic an middle east history but
I

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:07.762
did my first year at the school in

00:17:07.762 --> 00:17:10.421
Oriental and African studies in London
it's where I

00:17:10.421 --> 00:17:12.597
met my wife who graduated from Stanford
and

00:17:12.597 --> 00:17:15.450
was over at the llc and that's really
where.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:18.680
My true journalism career began, began
totally accidentally.

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:21.390
At the time it was 1975.

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:24.210
Jimmy Carter was running against Gerald
Ford for president.

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:29.430
And the campaign was in its heated stage
back home.

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:33.150
And Ann and I were walking down the street
in London during the campaign.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:34.950
And the Evening Standard, you're familiar

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:37.240
with the Evening Standard London after
newspaper.

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:39.240
They always have these blaring headlines
on the

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.340
newsstand to entice you to buy the paper.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:44.011
You know, Brad to Jen 'We're finished.'

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:48.450
You know, something about, the Royals or
whatever.

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:50.490
Well, this day we're walking down the
street.

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:53.900
The newstand had this headline on it.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:59.810
It said 'Carter to Jews' ; 'If elected I
promise to fire Doctor K.

00:18:01.870 --> 00:18:03.060
Now what was that about?

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:04.360
I sort of stopped and looked at it.

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:04.980
Point it out to him.

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:07.080
I said isn't that interesting?

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:08.220
Jimmy Carter's running for president.

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:11.079
He's trying to bring Jewish votes, and
he's doing it

00:18:11.079 --> 00:18:14.380
by promising to fire the first ever Jewish
Secretary of State.

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:16.560
Isn't that funny?

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:20.730
I've no idea what possessed me.

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:23.500
But I saw that headline, and I went back
to my

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:26.270
dorm room at London House and I wrote a
column about it.

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:28.990
What it meant.

00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:31.440
And my then girlfriend, now wife, who is

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:34.290
from Des Moines, Iowa, took that column
home over

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:36.770
vacation, gave it to Gilbert Cranberg,
then the

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:40.030
legendary editorial page editor of the Des
Moines Register.

00:18:40.030 --> 00:18:40.880
He liked it.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:47.150
He printed it on a half page of the Des
Moines Register under an Alf cartoon, and

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:54.320
they paid me $50 and I thought that was
the coolest thing in the whole world.

00:18:54.320 --> 00:18:56.870
I had been walking down a street.

00:18:56.870 --> 00:18:58.450
I saw something.

00:18:58.450 --> 00:19:00.070
I had an opinion about it.

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:04.039
I wrote it up and someone paid me $50.

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:07.280
And I was hooked ever after.

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:09.940
So my two years at Oxford, I wrote Op Ed
columns

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:12.270
for the Des Moines register in my own home
town, the

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:16.020
Minneapolis Star and Tribune all, some are
related to the middle

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:20.010
east, I got interested in the Middle East
also in high school.

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:24.880
And when i graduated from Oxford I applied
to jobs at AP and UPI.

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:26.060
The two wire services.

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:27.040
Competing wire services.

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:28.780
UPI doesn't exist anymore.

00:19:28.780 --> 00:19:34.940
AP looked at my, my clips, it consisted of
a dozen columns for Mid-West

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:39.650
newspapers and said, very interesting kid,
but you've never covered a fire.

00:19:39.650 --> 00:19:41.860
Let alone the city hall meeting.

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:43.290
Can't hire you.

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:44.960
But UPI being kind avis of wire service.

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:49.140
Had a great grizzled bureau chief back
then.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:49.960
Leon Daniel.

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:51.720
And he said, if you can write a column we
could

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:55.140
probably taint, chea/p, treat, train you
to write a news story.

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:59.130
And since you have studied Arabic and the
Iranian revolution

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:01.158
happened and they seem to have the same
squiggly letters.

00:20:01.158 --> 00:20:06.040
[SOUND] I think we'll hire you and give
you a chance.

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:09.820
So I actually started my career at UPI on
Fleet Street.

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:14.830
And to make a very long story short, I
worked

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:17.080
for them for two years covering the
Lebanese civil war.

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:17.530
Came home.

00:20:17.530 --> 00:20:18.920
Was hired by the New York Times.

00:20:20.046 --> 00:20:24.165
After a one hour emotional interview with
Abe Rosenthal where I explained why he

00:20:24.165 --> 00:20:28.300
could actually send a Jewish report to
Beirut because I had already been there.

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:31.750
The New York Times hired me, sent me back
to Beirut in April 1982.

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:33.440
Israel invaded six weeks later.

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:36.030
It became the biggest story in the world.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.418
I covered [UNKNOWN], the American embassy
bombing,

00:20:38.418 --> 00:20:40.558
the marine bombing, all these amazing
stories.

00:20:40.558 --> 00:20:44.289
My friend Phil Talbmann who's here came
out and actually joined me at one point.

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:48.190
I did that for, for two years, a little
more than two years.

00:20:48.190 --> 00:20:51.823
I was sent to Jerusalem, I was the
Jerusalem Bureau chief until

00:20:51.823 --> 00:20:55.711
1989, took a year off road from Beirut to
Jerusalem came back,

00:20:55.711 --> 00:20:59.728
and then was the State Department
correspondent for the New York Times

00:20:59.728 --> 00:21:03.875
beginning in 19 90 view with Jim Baker
thought it was a really.

00:21:03.875 --> 00:21:05.380
Sorry, '89 with Jim Baker.

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:07.220
Thought it was a really boring job, and it
made

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:11.080
a terrible mistake until this wall in
Berlin suddenly came down.

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:14.880
And suddenly I found myself with a front
row seat to the end of the cold war.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:19.400
Traveled 750 thousand miles with Baker as
the state department corresponded.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:21.870
Then I covered the first year of Bill
Clinton's White House.

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:23.820
That was Mr. Toad's Wild Ride.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:29.740
And, And then I did three years as the
chief economics correspondent.

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:33.370
And then, in January, 1995, they said,
you've done enough.

00:21:34.510 --> 00:21:37.240
We think, now, you might have some
opinions.

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:40.590
And they made me the foreign affairs
columnist.

00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:42.120
So, lets pick up the story there.

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:48.250
Because, as I say, everybody's writing
columns now, and I think

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:51.430
it's a wonderful thing.

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:54.840
But, the thing you need to understand
about

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:57.940
a column that's different from a news
story,.

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:01.410
Is that a column has to work.

00:22:02.990 --> 00:22:05.900
No one would ever say about a news story
that works or doesn't work.

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:07.601
That's very interesting flyer that's very

00:22:07.601 --> 00:22:09.980
interesting city hall meeting, very
interesting campaign.

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:13.620
News stories don't have to work, they
simply have to

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:16.130
tell you who, how, and where in a
compelling, interesting way.

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:18.546
But a column is different.

00:22:18.546 --> 00:22:22.370
The column has to work.

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:23.540
What makes a column work?

00:22:24.980 --> 00:22:29.730
What makes a column work is that it
produces a reaction.

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:31.720
News stories are meant to inform.

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:36.060
Columns are meant to produce a reaction.

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:38.170
So whether you're blogging or tweeting,
whether

00:22:38.170 --> 00:22:39.984
you're doing a TED talk or YouTube-ing.

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:44.667
You gotta have in your back of the mind,
in

00:22:44.667 --> 00:22:50.120
your mind, what is the reaction I'm
actually trying to produce?

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:53.740
And what I would argue is that there are
at least, I'm gonna give you nine.

00:22:53.740 --> 00:22:56.704
Nine kinds of reactions, and if you
produce

00:22:56.704 --> 00:22:59.356
any one of these nine, it's a column.

00:22:59.356 --> 00:23:02.820
So the first reaction you wanna produce.

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:06.000
Possibly is that the reader reads your
column,

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:07.970
puts it down and says, I didn't know that.

00:23:07.970 --> 00:23:08.830
I didn't know that.

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:13.850
It's good to break news in a column.

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:17.090
It's good to go places that others haven't
been.

00:23:17.090 --> 00:23:18.120
Share an insight.

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:20.230
Inform people about something.

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:24.600
It should always be in the service of an
idea and an opinion.

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:26.050
But if someone reads your column.

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:30.636
I, I did a column about a Arab B&amp;B

00:23:30.636 --> 00:23:34.950
a couple months ago and someone emailed me
afterwards.

00:23:34.950 --> 00:23:36.310
I really love this reaction.

00:23:36.310 --> 00:23:37.800
He just said, who knew?

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:40.140
Who knew.

00:23:40.140 --> 00:23:42.919
Who knew that 265 thousand people on New
Year's

00:23:42.919 --> 00:23:45.375
eve rented out rooms in their house around
the

00:23:45.375 --> 00:23:47.764
world to host someone on Arab B&amp;B That
begin

00:23:47.764 --> 00:23:52.000
the kick-off for writing about basically
the whole sharing economy.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:53.760
But if you read a column where someone
puts it

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:58.070
down and says I didn't know that, that's a
good column.

00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:00.010
Second, and this is a lot of what we do.

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:06.220
If someone reads your column and says, "I
never looked at it that

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:11.140
way, gosh thank you, you know I never ever
looked at it that way."

00:24:12.190 --> 00:24:15.180
That's definitely a successful column.

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:20.670
I have an Indian friend who edits the
Indian Express and we

00:24:20.670 --> 00:24:23.590
were talking a, a couple weeks ago and he
was telling me

00:24:23.590 --> 00:24:26.030
that he did a column and I would use this
in my

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:28.120
book as an example of I'd never looked at
it that way.

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:31.010
You remember there was a huge controversy
about an Indian

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:33.850
diplomat who had underpaid her maid in New
York City.

00:24:33.850 --> 00:24:38.970
And she was then, unceremoniously
arrested, and created a huge controversy.

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:41.020
And for weeks, it was all about the
diplomat,

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:43.540
and Indians rally to the diplomat, and
rally back.

00:24:43.540 --> 00:24:45.930
And he did a column about the maid.

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:48.700
Because everybody forgot about the maid.

00:24:48.700 --> 00:24:52.370
I thought that's a wonderful example of.

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:53.630
I never looked at it, that way.

00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:55.670
You gave new way to look at it.

00:24:56.870 --> 00:25:00.740
Your third kinda column, this is your
favorite, you'll live for this reaction.

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:03.360
Happens three or four times a year, if
you're lucky.

00:25:04.390 --> 00:25:09.390
You said, exactly what I felt, but didn't
know how to say.

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:12.300
God, God bless you, God bless you, my son!

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:12.960
God Bless you.

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:17.290
Oh, when that happens that's just, that is
so satisfying, when

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:23.280
you can actually articulate what people
are feeling but haven't yet said.

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:24.570
Enormously satisfying.

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:26.970
Fourth reaction is very simple.

00:25:26.970 --> 00:25:27.900
I want to kill you dead.

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:28.980
You annoy your offspring.

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:30.210
I want to waterboard you.

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:31.610
I want to beat you.

00:25:31.610 --> 00:25:34.280
I want, I've canceled my subscription.

00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:36.550
I absolutely hate you.

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:37.330
Okay.

00:25:37.330 --> 00:25:42.450
Your column is defined as much by people
who are against it as who are for it.

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:45.520
And if you don't have anyone against you,
er,

00:25:45.520 --> 00:25:49.150
chances are you don't have that many
people for you.

00:25:49.150 --> 00:25:52.860
And every once in a while, you just have
to roar back.

00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:54.400
And.

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:57.560
Act out.

00:25:57.560 --> 00:26:04.450
Peel off somebody's skin or the skin of an
issue in a rip roaring way.

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:05.450
Wouldn't do it every day.

00:26:05.450 --> 00:26:07.350
It can get a little old.

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:11.320
People can be exhausted by it, but every
once in awhile you need to do that.

00:26:12.330 --> 00:26:15.580
It's very important though,I believe, I
practice.

00:26:15.580 --> 00:26:17.180
Hunt big game.

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:17.770
Hunt big game.

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:23.490
Don't pick on little people and don't pick
on little ideas.

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:24.290
Hunt big game.

00:26:25.910 --> 00:26:30.800
The fifth reaction and this one is really
hard.

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:33.166
Don't try this trick at home kids.

00:26:33.166 --> 00:26:36.600
You made me laugh you made me cry.

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:39.040
That's a great if you can make people
really

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:42.080
laugh like my college Dale Collins if you
can

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:45.760
write about an emotional moment or that
drives an

00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:49.280
opinion and make them cry that's a very
successful column.

00:26:49.280 --> 00:26:50.940
When it's done well it's awesome.

00:26:52.070 --> 00:26:55.950
When it's done badly it's cringe inducing
okay.

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:57.520
Being, try to be funny.

00:26:57.520 --> 00:26:58.940
And not being funny.

00:26:58.940 --> 00:27:05.440
Or trying to be sentimental, and not
actually touching people is awful.

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:06.000
Okay.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:08.640
So, I don't do this very often, but every
once

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:10.540
in a while when you can do it, it really
worked.

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:17.420
Six reactions, you're calm - this is also
very important for people to remember.

00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:20.890
Is when someone reads in your comments as
you said before,

00:27:20.890 --> 00:27:25.330
as you said before, you have to repeat
yourself in this business.

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:28.354
Maybe you didn't read my Wednesday column
when I talked about this, maybe

00:27:28.354 --> 00:27:32.130
you didn't see my Sunday column, maybe you
were out of town or traveling.

00:27:32.130 --> 00:27:36.480
If you believe in an issue, you've gotta
drive it,.

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:41.030
And you've got to invent a language that
will stick in people's minds.

00:27:42.560 --> 00:27:45.760
I often get criticized, I get criticized
for many things.

00:27:45.760 --> 00:27:49.470
But one is, he's repeating himself again,
there he goes.

00:27:49.470 --> 00:27:51.210
Oh, I do that deliberately.

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:54.970
Cause I, if you're gonna hate me, I really
want you to understand why.

00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:55.740
Okay?

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:58.070
And I want you to never forget it, you
know.

00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:00.660
I'm a big believer that to name something
is to own it.

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:02.700
If you can name an issue, you can own the
issue.

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:03.605
The world is flat.

00:28:03.605 --> 00:28:06.020
Green is the new red, white, and blue.

00:28:06.020 --> 00:28:07.820
So I spent a lot of time naming things.

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:10.420
I wanna stick in your head or in your
craw.

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:11.520
Either place is fine.

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:16.470
But I don't hesitate to repeat myself
because of that.

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:17.710
Obviously, in different ways.

00:28:19.120 --> 00:28:20.019
Seven is.

00:28:21.090 --> 00:28:25.230
This is really, really important reaction
to get from people.

00:28:25.230 --> 00:28:27.010
You challenge me, you disappointed me.

00:28:27.010 --> 00:28:29.930
Now what is this about?

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:34.840
I really believe it's important as a
columnist to challenge your base.

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:39.760
It's for liberal columnists to every once
in a while challenge liberals.

00:28:39.760 --> 00:28:42.740
It's for conservatives to challenge
conservatives

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:47.480
never let anyone take you for granted.

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:49.730
Never let anyone feel they own you.

00:28:50.990 --> 00:28:53.970
And when you do that when you anger and

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:57.600
rile your own base you will hear from
them.

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:02.070
But I think it's extremely important as a
columnist to check yourself.

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:05.710
By challenging your own base and a lot of
your own theories.

00:29:07.510 --> 00:29:11.160
Eight, this is the one reaction you want
to avoid.

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:15.480
I knew what you were going to say by
reading your byline.

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:20.420
Oh that's death as a columnist.

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:22.740
That, that, that goes to the columnist who
never challenged their own base.

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:30.140
Where you become so predictable left,
right or centered that people

00:29:30.140 --> 00:29:34.960
know what you're or think they know what
you're gonna say just by reading you bio.

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:37.980
That is definitely to be avoided.

00:29:39.870 --> 00:29:41.980
And the last reaction,.

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:47.004
And, this happens rarely, but it's, I
think, a very important

00:29:47.004 --> 00:29:51.416
one, is I bet that column didn't take very
long to write.

00:29:51.416 --> 00:29:55.716
[LAUGH] Now, that's actually a compliment.

00:29:55.716 --> 00:29:57.888
I'll tell you why.

00:29:57.888 --> 00:30:01.099
The best columns, the very best columns I

00:30:01.099 --> 00:30:06.340
feel I've written, came from some really
deep place.

00:30:06.340 --> 00:30:10.313
And they came out so fast, and so quickly,
that it took me as

00:30:10.313 --> 00:30:14.610
long as it took my fingers to hit the
keys, to write that column.

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:19.590
So when someone can feel almost that, that
column just

00:30:19.590 --> 00:30:23.970
came straight out from some deep place,
that's a real compliment.

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:28.066
So those are, very quickly, the kind of
nine

00:30:28.066 --> 00:30:32.730
reactions I'm looking for, as a columnist,
when I'm writing.

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:37.460
And if I produce any one of those nine, I
think I've got a column.

00:30:39.570 --> 00:30:42.481
Now just quickly, before we go to
questions

00:30:42.481 --> 00:30:45.392
in about 10, 15 minutes let me just

00:30:45.392 --> 00:30:50.587
share with you some of the things I've
learned in the 20 years of doing this,

00:30:50.587 --> 00:30:55.937
some of the lessons that I take away from
writing a column, and having been able to

00:30:55.937 --> 00:31:01.270
travel around and see so many conflicts
and meet people and issues face to face.

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:05.906
I'd say the number one lesson that, that
I've learned is that,

00:31:05.906 --> 00:31:09.867
and people who read me know that this
appears in my column

00:31:09.867 --> 00:31:13.743
every so often, is that in the history of
the world, in

00:31:13.743 --> 00:31:18.330
the history of all mankind, no one has
ever washed a rented car.

00:31:20.140 --> 00:31:21.400
I'm a huge believer in that.

00:31:21.400 --> 00:31:25.895
It's, it's one of my, just a guiding
principles in life, okay, that

00:31:25.895 --> 00:31:29.890
in the history of the world, no one has
ever washed a rented car.

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:33.860
Someone always raises their hand and says,
I washed my rented car.

00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:36.240
But, I think you know what I mean.

00:31:36.240 --> 00:31:40.030
And first learned that from Larry Summers,
actually,

00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:43.460
but I'm a big believer that ownership is
everything.

00:31:45.020 --> 00:31:48.595
When people have a sense of ownership of
their company, when kids

00:31:48.595 --> 00:31:52.755
have a sense of ownership over their
education, when teachers have sense of

00:31:52.755 --> 00:31:57.240
ownership over their classroom, and most
importantly, when people have a sense

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:02.630
of ownership of their country and its
future, really good, good things happen.

00:32:02.630 --> 00:32:07.540
And when people don't feel a sense of
ownership, bad things happen.

00:32:07.540 --> 00:32:10.760
All these color revolutions we're seeing,
all these

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:13.560
Arab springs, are really about people who
have

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:17.060
been renting their countries from colonial
powers, kings

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:20.846
and dictators, their whole lives, and now
taking ownership.

00:32:20.846 --> 00:32:22.950
And it's a beautiful thing to see.

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:29.153
But ownership is really the key to life,
because when people own

00:32:29.153 --> 00:32:34.780
things, when, when people own their
companies, their future, their

00:32:34.780 --> 00:32:40.700
classroom, they will always do so much
more then you can ever ask them to do.

00:32:40.700 --> 00:32:44.170
And when they don't have the sense of
ownership, bad things really happen.

00:32:45.260 --> 00:32:47.140
And that is a lesson I've seen, whether

00:32:47.140 --> 00:32:50.250
I'm covering education or whether I'm
covering Tahrir Square.

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:55.020
Second thing I've really learned is that
there's two kind of editors.

00:32:57.300 --> 00:32:59.597
There's editors who when you come to them
with a story

00:32:59.597 --> 00:33:02.670
idea, they say, no that's, that, that,
that just doesn't work.

00:33:03.830 --> 00:33:06.470
And there's editors who say, yes but.

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:12.510
Be a yes but person, because when someone
comes to you with a story idea

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:14.869
and you say, no that's never going to

00:33:14.869 --> 00:33:18.640
work, they just go away depressed and
defeated.

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:21.217
When you say to them, yes, but that is
such a

00:33:21.217 --> 00:33:24.212
great idea, but if you just make the third
graph the

00:33:24.212 --> 00:33:26.998
lead, and you take the sixth graph and
move it up

00:33:26.998 --> 00:33:31.750
the third, you give people a sense of
ownership over that story.

00:33:31.750 --> 00:33:37.667
Man, they will take any piece of advice
from you, and they will run with that

00:33:37.667 --> 00:33:43.960
story again, so much farther and faster
than anything you could demand of them.

00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:45.380
Be a yes but teacher.

00:33:45.380 --> 00:33:47.130
Be a yes but parent.

00:33:47.130 --> 00:33:48.030
Be a yes but editor.

00:33:49.600 --> 00:33:53.458
Third thing I learned is that the two most
important

00:33:53.458 --> 00:33:58.730
words in foreign policy are self and
sustaining, self sustaining.

00:33:58.730 --> 00:34:00.942
We are really good America, I've covered a
lot of this so

00:34:00.942 --> 00:34:04.000
something I've learned the hard way, we
are really good at breaking things.

00:34:05.070 --> 00:34:06.645
We are really good at breaking things.

00:34:06.645 --> 00:34:08.800
There's no country we cannot break around
the world.

00:34:09.810 --> 00:34:10.930
We've demonstrated that.

00:34:13.110 --> 00:34:14.810
We are not good at building things.

00:34:14.810 --> 00:34:17.410
We're like a football team with an offense
and no defense.

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:20.590
To put it another way

00:34:23.990 --> 00:34:26.410
we, we can stop bad things from happening

00:34:26.410 --> 00:34:29.980
in places, but we cannot make good things
happen.

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:31.440
That is the lesson I've taken away

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:34.390
from Bosnia, and Libya, and Iraq, and
Afghanistan.

00:34:36.050 --> 00:34:41.000
To make a reality self sustaining is the
real challenge.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:45.271
The real challenge is not what we do when
we are there, not what

00:34:45.271 --> 00:34:50.460
surge we mount, but what we leave behind
and what is sustained after we go.

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:51.200
It doesn't matter if you're talking about
war,

00:34:51.200 --> 00:34:54.160
doesn't matter if you're talking about
doing an

00:34:54.160 --> 00:34:56.700
education project somewhere, doesn't
matter if you're talking

00:34:56.700 --> 00:35:00.080
about trying to start or invest in a
company.

00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:04.040
It's what is self sustaining is all that
really matters.

00:35:04.040 --> 00:35:07.969
And it's unfortunately, I've seen too many
examples of

00:35:07.969 --> 00:35:11.910
non self sustaining in the foreign policy
I've covered.

00:35:13.250 --> 00:35:17.059
Another big lesson I've learned, and I
come across this all the time,

00:35:17.059 --> 00:35:21.710
is that humiliation, in foreign policy, is
the single most powerful human emotion.

00:35:23.420 --> 00:35:30.220
And, I've seen the impact of people
feeling humiliated and what they do.

00:35:30.220 --> 00:35:34.150
You know, take away people's money,
they'll be angry, they'll be depressed.

00:35:35.710 --> 00:35:40.156
Put them in jail, beat them, hurt them,
you'll get a backlash.

00:35:40.156 --> 00:35:45.240
But humiliate someone, humiliate someone
and they'll wrap themselves

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:48.690
in dynamite, and blow you up or your twin
towers.

00:35:50.300 --> 00:35:53.920
So much of what I cover in the world goes
back to humiliation.

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:59.186
Whether you're talking about Ukrainians
and Russians, Japanese and Chinese

00:35:59.186 --> 00:36:02.407
or Arab Muslims in the Middle East and
America, I

00:36:02.407 --> 00:36:06.414
feel I benefit most in covering foreign
policy from whatever

00:36:06.414 --> 00:36:12.020
I've read about psychology, and whatever
I've read about humiliation.

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:16.966
It is the single most important emotion,
and it drives more foreign

00:36:16.966 --> 00:36:22.360
policy issues than any other issue,
resources, money, nationalism.

00:36:25.100 --> 00:36:26.290
Another big lesson I've learned.

00:36:26.290 --> 00:36:29.787
I've alluded to the fact that I'm a caddy
and I'm a golfer,

00:36:29.787 --> 00:36:33.780
and there are also just two kind of
golfers and two kind of countries.

00:36:34.930 --> 00:36:38.057
So, Tom Watson had a famous caddy, Bruce
Edwards,

00:36:38.057 --> 00:36:41.550
he tragically just died, died of Lou
Gehrig's disease.

00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:43.602
But he caddied for Tom Watson his, most of
his

00:36:43.602 --> 00:36:46.880
career except for three years, he caddied
for Greg Norman.

00:36:46.880 --> 00:36:48.860
Then he went back to caddy for Tom Watson.

00:36:48.860 --> 00:36:51.360
Afterwards, he did an interview and people
asked him

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:54.880
what was the difference between Greg
Norman and Tom Watson.

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:58.435
And he said, very simple, you caddy for
both these guys, and both

00:36:58.435 --> 00:37:02.860
their balls they'd hit a perfect drive
down the middle of the fairway.

00:37:02.860 --> 00:37:05.430
And each guy's ball would end in, end up
in a divot.

00:37:06.770 --> 00:37:09.624
Greg Norman would say, my luck, I hit a
perfect drive.

00:37:09.624 --> 00:37:12.450
If I didn't have bad luck, I'd have no
luck at all.

00:37:12.450 --> 00:37:13.370
Look at this.

00:37:13.370 --> 00:37:15.280
I'm in the, I hit this drive perfectly.

00:37:16.650 --> 00:37:21.410
And Tom Watson would say, Bruce, watch
this.

00:37:23.140 --> 00:37:25.353
There are really just two kind of golfers,
and

00:37:25.353 --> 00:37:27.580
the truth is there just two kind of
countries.

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:33.040
There are those who say we're behind,
let's figure out what's

00:37:33.040 --> 00:37:38.430
the best way to get ahead, the Meiji
restoration in Japan.

00:37:39.750 --> 00:37:42.960
And there's those who say we're behind,
what did you do to me.

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:49.274
And there's a lot of countries and a lot
of people, who waste a lot of time

00:37:49.274 --> 00:37:52.720
and energy complaining about being in the
divot, rather

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:55.051
than figuring out how to get out of it.

00:37:55.051 --> 00:37:59.800
And I unfortunately have to spend a lot of
my time covering those countries.

00:38:03.150 --> 00:38:05.990
Another big lesson I learned covering Jim
Baker, actually, Secretary of

00:38:05.990 --> 00:38:10.710
State, is that success breeds authority,
and authority breeds more success.

00:38:10.710 --> 00:38:13.620
And I learned this in a funny way.

00:38:13.620 --> 00:38:16.830
I, I traveled with Baker on all 15 trips
he took

00:38:16.830 --> 00:38:19.460
to the Middle East to put together the
Madrid peace process.

00:38:20.990 --> 00:38:26.442
And on 14 of those trips, I came home and
wrote Secretary of State James A Baker,

00:38:26.442 --> 00:38:31.750
III failed again to achieve his objective
of putting together a peace process.

00:38:31.750 --> 00:38:33.260
And then trip 15, he succeeded.

00:38:33.260 --> 00:38:35.782
He came back and stuck his finger in my
face

00:38:35.782 --> 00:38:39.690
and said, Freedman, you're going to write
that I succeeded.

00:38:41.350 --> 00:38:43.730
And he spiced that with a few other things
as well.

00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:47.940
So we went off to Madrid and he,

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:51.970
he, convened the first, basically, region
wide peace conference.

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:55.269
It was a remarkable diplomatic achievement
and we actually flew

00:38:55.269 --> 00:38:59.900
from Madrid, I never forgotten this, to
Nagorno, to, to, Azerbaijan.

00:38:59.900 --> 00:39:02.230
And, he give a press conference in
Azerbaijan.

00:39:02.230 --> 00:39:03.320
He'd just come from Madrid.

00:39:03.320 --> 00:39:07.020
It was a world shaking story, covered all
over the world.

00:39:07.020 --> 00:39:10.585
He stands up at the press conference in
Azerbaijan.

00:39:10.585 --> 00:39:13.528
As every reporter stands up, first
question,

00:39:13.528 --> 00:39:15.808
Mr. Baker, in light of what you've

00:39:15.808 --> 00:39:18.308
achieved in Madrid, would you come here

00:39:18.308 --> 00:39:21.670
and mediate now between our meninas and
azuries.

00:39:21.670 --> 00:39:22.210
And I was a pool reporter.

00:39:24.590 --> 00:39:25.410
Baker winked at me.

00:39:25.410 --> 00:39:25.779
His eyes rolled.

00:39:25.779 --> 00:39:28.137
The last thing he was going to do

00:39:28.137 --> 00:39:33.590
was mediate between Armenia and Azerbaijan
over Nagorno-Karabakh.

00:39:33.590 --> 00:39:37.262
But the lesson I took away from that, was
how quickly his success in

00:39:37.262 --> 00:39:39.952
Madrid had translated into more authority,
and

00:39:39.952 --> 00:39:43.170
more authority gave him leverage for more
success.

00:39:43.170 --> 00:39:47.706
I think it's true whatever you do, small
victories, small victories can

00:39:47.706 --> 00:39:52.250
add up to greater authority, and the
authority for really big victories.

00:39:55.994 --> 00:39:59.935
Another really important lesson that I've
learned is that

00:39:59.935 --> 00:40:04.530
there is a huge, huge difference between
skepticism and cynicism.

00:40:04.530 --> 00:40:09.025
This is a really important issue for me
because unfortunately a lot of

00:40:09.025 --> 00:40:11.418
people in my business have forgot where

00:40:11.418 --> 00:40:14.940
the line is between skepticism and
cynicism.

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:16.892
Skepticism is about asking questions,
being

00:40:16.892 --> 00:40:19.060
dubious, be wary, not being gullible.

00:40:19.060 --> 00:40:21.761
Cynicism is about already having the
answers, or at least

00:40:21.761 --> 00:40:24.470
thinking you already have them, about a
person or event.

00:40:24.470 --> 00:40:26.638
The sceptics says, I don't think that's
true.

00:40:26.638 --> 00:40:27.890
I'm going to check it out.

00:40:27.890 --> 00:40:29.456
The cynic says I know that's not true.

00:40:29.456 --> 00:40:30.074
It couldn't be.

00:40:30.074 --> 00:40:31.954
He couldn't have done that for those
reasons.

00:40:31.954 --> 00:40:33.060
I'm going to slam him or her.

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:35.432
The sceptic is in search of the truth.

00:40:35.432 --> 00:40:37.430
The cynic is in search of snark.

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:42.500
There is such a thing, there is such a
thing as healthy scepticism.

00:40:42.500 --> 00:40:44.380
There is no such thing as healthy
cynicism.

00:40:45.380 --> 00:40:48.416
The cynic is always prejudging, and
prejudging is the

00:40:48.416 --> 00:40:51.850
enemy of inquiry and learning, which
requires an open mind.

00:40:53.320 --> 00:40:56.550
The truth is most of the journalists I've
ever known were not cynics.

00:40:56.550 --> 00:40:57.840
Just the opposite, most of them are

00:40:57.840 --> 00:41:01.100
rather idealistic, with a certain
underlying crusading spirit.

00:41:01.100 --> 00:41:03.501
But cynic, cynicism is present enough in

00:41:03.501 --> 00:41:07.360
our craft that it's worth constantly
guarding against.

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:10.007
If we want to be taken seriously, we have
to take our

00:41:10.007 --> 00:41:13.960
subjects seriously, and that means doing
more than just sneering at them.

00:41:15.760 --> 00:41:20.970
I learned this lesson early on in my
career in a odd series of events.

00:41:20.970 --> 00:41:25.660
I was hired from UPI and joined the New
York Times business day staff.

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:27.840
That was where they, only job they had to
stash me in

00:41:27.840 --> 00:41:30.190
at the paper when I came to New York, back
in 1981.

00:41:30.190 --> 00:41:35.240
And on the staff, business day, was a
young editor named Nathaniel Nash.

00:41:36.640 --> 00:41:38.330
And Nathaniel was a born again Christian.

00:41:38.330 --> 00:41:40.140
He was deeply interested in the Holy Land.

00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:46.029
Because I had covered the Middle East all
those years, we used to go out to lunch,

00:41:46.029 --> 00:41:51.330
and he loved to hear about Israel and what
was going on in the Middle East.

00:41:51.330 --> 00:41:55.590
So in May of 1982, sorry in April, when
the Times sent me off to,

00:41:55.590 --> 00:41:58.670
to Beirut to be their correspondent, the
business

00:41:58.670 --> 00:42:00.390
day staff had a goodbye party for me.

00:42:01.700 --> 00:42:04.198
And Nathaniel pulled me aside at the end
of the

00:42:04.198 --> 00:42:06.530
party and he said, I'm going to pray for
you.

00:42:08.070 --> 00:42:10.020
I'm going to pray for your safety.

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:14.760
And I said Nathaniel, I so appreciate
that.

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:20.010
And I always considered it my lucky charm
when I was in Beirut.

00:42:20.010 --> 00:42:23.059
And when I got out of Beirut, first day I

00:42:23.059 --> 00:42:28.450
got out, I called Nathaniel and, and I
thanked him.

00:42:28.450 --> 00:42:32.040
Now the book on Nathaniel, as a reporter,
was that he was too nice.

00:42:33.130 --> 00:42:34.750
His colleagues always doubted that anyone

00:42:34.750 --> 00:42:37.560
that nice could ever succeed in
journalism.

00:42:37.560 --> 00:42:40.220
But somehow he triumphed over this
handicap.

00:42:40.220 --> 00:42:42.077
And he went from one successful assignment

00:42:42.077 --> 00:42:44.250
in business day, to Latin America, to
another.

00:42:44.250 --> 00:42:47.741
And it was because Nathaniel intuitively
understood, I

00:42:47.741 --> 00:42:51.970
always thought, the difference between
skepticism and cynicism.

00:42:51.970 --> 00:42:54.298
I only wish I could have done for him what
he

00:42:54.298 --> 00:42:57.628
did for me, because Nathaniel Nash, age
44, was the one

00:42:57.628 --> 00:43:01.957
reporter on Commerce Secretary Ron Brown's
airplane, when it crashed into

00:43:01.957 --> 00:43:06.110
a Croatian hillside on April 3, 1996,
killing everyone on board.

00:43:08.130 --> 00:43:09.510
I was devastated.

00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:13.403
With today's cacophony of magazine shows,
Oprah style interview sessions, and

00:43:13.403 --> 00:43:17.480
talking heads, many people have lost sight
of what real journalists do.

00:43:17.480 --> 00:43:20.400
Journalists do not appear on cable TV to
scream at each other.

00:43:20.400 --> 00:43:24.070
Real journalists don't interview mothers
and daughters on daytime confession hours.

00:43:24.070 --> 00:43:26.940
Real journalists don't have their own
shock jock radio shows.

00:43:26.940 --> 00:43:30.815
Unfortunately, those are the people many
American see most in quasi-journalistic

00:43:30.815 --> 00:43:34.640
roles, and these are people they think of
as journalists today.

00:43:34.640 --> 00:43:37.181
Journalists are reporters like Nathaniel
Nash, who

00:43:37.181 --> 00:43:39.270
go off to uncomfortable and often
dangerous

00:43:39.270 --> 00:43:42.882
places, like Croatia, and get on a
military plane to chase after a visiting

00:43:42.882 --> 00:43:46.663
dignitary, without giving it a second
thought, all to get a few fresh quotes,

00:43:46.663 --> 00:43:50.840
maybe a scoop, or even just a paragraph of
color, that no one else has.

00:43:50.840 --> 00:43:54.755
When I first heard the news about
Nathaniel, I whispered a prayer

00:43:54.755 --> 00:43:58.454
for him, but I am sure that wasn't
necessary, for he was such

00:43:58.454 --> 00:44:03.081
a good soul, who knew the difference
between skepticism and cynicism, that

00:44:03.081 --> 00:44:07.380
I know he is sitting at God's elbow,
probably taking notes right now.

00:44:07.380 --> 00:44:09.124
Another big lesson for me is most

00:44:09.124 --> 00:44:12.230
people listen through their stomachs, not
their ears.

00:44:13.270 --> 00:44:16.450
Really, most people do not listen through
their ears.

00:44:16.450 --> 00:44:18.550
They listen through their stomachs.

00:44:18.550 --> 00:44:22.810
You connect with someone on a gut level,
as a reporter, as a columnist, or as a

00:44:22.810 --> 00:44:25.394
leader, you don't have to bother them with
the

00:44:25.394 --> 00:44:29.010
details at all, they know they've
connected with you.

00:44:29.010 --> 00:44:30.924
You don't connect with someone on a gut

00:44:30.924 --> 00:44:33.360
level, and you cannot show them enough
details.

00:44:34.570 --> 00:44:37.735
Who remembers Anwar Sadat's speech, before
the

00:44:37.735 --> 00:44:41.590
Knesset, after he flew from Cairo to
Jerusalem?

00:44:41.590 --> 00:44:41.900
Nobody.

00:44:41.900 --> 00:44:45.221
It was actually one of the harshest
speeches ever

00:44:45.221 --> 00:44:48.875
given by an Arab leader about the
Palestinian cause.

00:44:48.875 --> 00:44:51.638
No one in Israel remembers he even gave a

00:44:51.638 --> 00:44:56.181
speech, because Sadat connected with
Israelis on a gut level.

00:44:56.181 --> 00:45:01.590
And once he did, they did not care about
the details.

00:45:01.590 --> 00:45:05.740
How do you connect with people on the gut
level?

00:45:05.740 --> 00:45:08.320
I think it's by doing things that are
hard.

00:45:08.320 --> 00:45:09.775
That are visibly hard and by taking a
risk.

00:45:09.775 --> 00:45:12.388
[UNKNOWN] never connected with Israelis on
a gut

00:45:12.388 --> 00:45:15.671
level, [UNKNOWN] has never connected with
Palestinians on

00:45:15.671 --> 00:45:17.748
a gut level, and that's why each one

00:45:17.748 --> 00:45:21.390
is always asking the other for one more
detail.

00:45:21.390 --> 00:45:22.710
Cuz there is no gut connection.

00:45:25.205 --> 00:45:26.260
gettin' here toward the end.

00:45:26.260 --> 00:45:27.870
I just a few more.

00:45:27.870 --> 00:45:33.390
I would say, one of the biggest things
I've learned

00:45:33.390 --> 00:45:36.950
from covering the Arab Spring is really
how right Lennon was.

00:45:36.950 --> 00:45:42.410
That if you don't have an organization
you've got nothing.

00:45:42.410 --> 00:45:44.511
It's great to have a democracy movement

00:45:44.511 --> 00:45:46.990
and talk really square or [UNKNOWN] and
[UNKNOWN].

00:45:48.100 --> 00:45:51.220
But organization really is everything.

00:45:51.220 --> 00:45:54.009
If you cannot translate your movement as
an organization

00:45:54.009 --> 00:45:57.780
that can contest an election and deliver
your politics.

00:45:57.780 --> 00:46:01.200
I cover a lot of people who haven't done
that, and that's

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:04.420
really been the theme of everything I've
been writing the last few years.

00:46:07.240 --> 00:46:10.390
Professor at Columbia, Mark Mazower, wrote
this, I really like this.

00:46:10.390 --> 00:46:12.938
He said that anger at corruption has
fueled number rallies all

00:46:12.938 --> 00:46:15.700
over the past all over the world, over the
past few years.

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:17.940
Especially as austerity and high
unemployment have sharpened

00:46:17.940 --> 00:46:19.790
the divides between haves and the have
nots.

00:46:19.790 --> 00:46:23.000
Demonstrations however are an effective
means of ending corruption.

00:46:23.000 --> 00:46:25.690
That takes institutional transformation.

00:46:25.690 --> 00:46:27.240
A long slow slog.

00:46:27.240 --> 00:46:29.490
Mostly invisible and untheatrical.

00:46:29.490 --> 00:46:32.045
It's the opposite of street protest who's
power lies

00:46:32.045 --> 00:46:35.620
in being as public, dramatic, and
instantaneous as possible.

00:46:35.620 --> 00:46:37.210
What crowds in downtown [UNKNOWN] Kiev
have

00:46:37.210 --> 00:46:39.730
in common was their extraordinary
diversity, but

00:46:39.730 --> 00:46:41.470
this very quality also explains why they

00:46:41.470 --> 00:46:44.550
have been incapable of sustaining a
political future.

00:46:44.550 --> 00:46:47.150
The fundamental [UNKNOWN] insight still
holds,

00:46:47.150 --> 00:46:50.370
nothing can be done without an
organization.

00:46:50.370 --> 00:46:55.290
I've just been covering a lot of people
over the last three years in particular,.

00:46:55.290 --> 00:46:56.530
We really need to learn that lesson.

00:46:58.200 --> 00:47:00.630
Let me just close with, with a few more
personal ones.

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:08.200
One is about America and maybe a little
goes back to Minnesota, and that is that

00:47:09.640 --> 00:47:13.940
pluralism, especially the pluralism we
have in this country, I see in this room,.

00:47:15.410 --> 00:47:19.000
Pluralism is such an incredible thing.

00:47:21.040 --> 00:47:23.060
And America is such a

00:47:25.560 --> 00:47:25.710
freak.

00:47:25.710 --> 00:47:26.930
We are freaks.

00:47:28.450 --> 00:47:31.290
We have twice elected a black man who's
middle name

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:33.930
is Hussein who defeated a woman to run
against the Mormon.

00:47:35.070 --> 00:47:36.130
Who does that?

00:47:36.130 --> 00:47:37.130
Okay.

00:47:37.130 --> 00:47:38.780
Who does that?

00:47:38.780 --> 00:47:39.530
Nobody does that.

00:47:43.620 --> 00:47:46.690
What we have in this country is so unique.

00:47:46.690 --> 00:47:47.190
And

00:47:48.990 --> 00:47:50.550
we treat it like it's a football.

00:47:51.940 --> 00:47:54.580
We kick this country around like it's a
football.

00:47:56.070 --> 00:47:56.570
It's

00:47:58.270 --> 00:47:59.340
not a football.

00:47:59.340 --> 00:48:01.676
It's a faberge egg.

00:48:01.676 --> 00:48:05.180
We can drop it.

00:48:05.180 --> 00:48:06.300
We can break it.

00:48:06.300 --> 00:48:10.880
And in the interconnected, interdependent
world we live in.

00:48:10.880 --> 00:48:12.300
To have mastered.

00:48:13.540 --> 00:48:14.490
Pluralism?

00:48:14.490 --> 00:48:16.040
Oh, and we're still a work in progress.

00:48:16.040 --> 00:48:19.050
You need only look at the racism directed
at Obama to know that.

00:48:19.050 --> 00:48:20.900
We are still a work in progress.

00:48:20.900 --> 00:48:22.350
But to have gotten this far.

00:48:22.350 --> 00:48:22.850
That,

00:48:25.050 --> 00:48:27.080
I know you learned about comparative
advantage today.

00:48:28.090 --> 00:48:31.310
That is the single most comparative
advantage.

00:48:31.310 --> 00:48:34.620
We have as a country in the world.

00:48:34.620 --> 00:48:37.820
And, we really need to take care of that

00:48:41.710 --> 00:48:43.110
we need to appreciate it for what it is.

00:48:43.110 --> 00:48:45.230
Big lesson that's really sustained me, I'm
a little Jewish guy

00:48:45.230 --> 00:48:50.810
from Minnesota who spent the last 30 years
covering the Arab world.

00:48:52.198 --> 00:48:57.730
And as anyone who read my copy knows, I
don't do it by going

00:48:57.730 --> 00:49:02.200
around telling people how wonderful they
are.

00:49:02.200 --> 00:49:04.580
How do you, how do you survive?

00:49:04.580 --> 00:49:05.990
In that kind of environment?

00:49:05.990 --> 00:49:07.520
As a journalist and then a columnist.

00:49:07.520 --> 00:49:08.530
And tell the truth?

00:49:09.600 --> 00:49:13.270
And to me it's one overriding thing I've
learned.

00:49:13.270 --> 00:49:16.760
My survival mechanism being a good
listener.

00:49:16.760 --> 00:49:18.220
Listening is the key to life.

00:49:19.330 --> 00:49:23.150
Not only because of what you learn when
you listen but something more important.

00:49:23.150 --> 00:49:26.390
When you actually listen to people don't
just wait for them to stop

00:49:26.390 --> 00:49:32.050
talking, but actually listen to them, it's
amazing what they'll let you say back.

00:49:33.110 --> 00:49:35.580
Because listening is a sign of respect.

00:49:36.850 --> 00:49:40.230
And when you truly listen to people, it's
a

00:49:40.230 --> 00:49:43.170
amazing what they will let you say,
because if people

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:47.360
think, you actually respect them, and that
you want a

00:49:47.360 --> 00:49:50.270
good future for them, they will let you
say anything.

00:49:50.270 --> 00:49:52.610
But if they smell you're not listening,.

00:49:52.610 --> 00:49:55.090
That you really don't care what happens to
them.

00:49:55.090 --> 00:49:58.150
You cannot tell them the sun is shining.

00:49:59.510 --> 00:50:01.530
Something Dick Cheney and George Bush
never understood.

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:09.326
As much as they wanna liberate Iraq,
people there sensed, that they

00:50:09.326 --> 00:50:16.380
really didn't respect them, and they
certainly weren't listening.

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:17.720
We're coming to the end here.

00:50:17.720 --> 00:50:18.220
Couple

00:50:20.330 --> 00:50:20.450
more.

00:50:20.450 --> 00:50:22.680
Never drink the whole milkshake.

00:50:30.610 --> 00:50:33.750
Especially never drink all of the other
guy's

00:50:36.200 --> 00:50:36.470
milkshake.

00:50:36.470 --> 00:50:39.270
One of my favorite pieces of movie dialog
from political science

00:50:39.270 --> 00:50:42.190
is, you know the end of the film, There
Will be Blood,

00:50:42.190 --> 00:50:46.290
the 2007 classic about, the ruthless
silver mining turned oilman, might have

00:50:46.290 --> 00:50:50.630
turned oilman who could not stop at
anything to accumulate more wealth.

00:50:50.630 --> 00:50:53.600
And you remember when Daniel Plainview,
the, the

00:50:53.600 --> 00:50:58.910
oil driller basically takes Paul Dano, Eli
Sunday aside.

00:50:58.910 --> 00:51:01.410
And Eli Sunday wants to sell him some more
land that he's

00:51:01.410 --> 00:51:05.900
sure has oil and he says, drainage Eli,
drainage, Eli, you boy.

00:51:05.900 --> 00:51:07.100
Drained it dry.

00:51:07.100 --> 00:51:08.020
Sorry.

00:51:08.020 --> 00:51:11.590
Here if you have a milkshake, and I have a
milkshake, and I have a straw.

00:51:11.590 --> 00:51:12.070
There it is.

00:51:12.070 --> 00:51:12.900
There's the straw.

00:51:12.900 --> 00:51:13.560
You see.

00:51:13.560 --> 00:51:14.460
You watching.

00:51:14.460 --> 00:51:17.360
And my star reaches across the room and

00:51:17.360 --> 00:51:18.472
starts to drink your milkshake, I drink
your milkshake.

00:51:18.472 --> 00:51:19.220
Your whole milkshake.

00:51:19.220 --> 00:51:19.870
I drink

00:51:25.830 --> 00:51:25.990
it up.

00:51:25.990 --> 00:51:28.330
Man, if there's one lesson I've learned
from covering the Arab Spring.

00:51:28.330 --> 00:51:32.420
It's do not try to drink the other guys
whole milkshake.

00:51:33.750 --> 00:51:36.220
This is about no victor, no vanquished.

00:51:36.220 --> 00:51:38.940
If you don't leave space for the other
people, whether

00:51:38.940 --> 00:51:42.850
it's Ukrainians leaving space for the
Russian speakers among them.

00:51:42.850 --> 00:51:44.520
Whether it's Sunnis leaving space for

00:51:44.520 --> 00:51:46.860
Shiites or Shiites leaving space for
Sunnis.

00:51:46.860 --> 00:51:52.540
If you want it all and try to get it all,
you will end up with nothing.

00:51:52.540 --> 00:51:55.430
The Arab spring that has been done the
best so far.

00:51:55.430 --> 00:52:01.860
Interesting the one we have had the least
to do with,Tunisia.

00:52:01.860 --> 00:52:01.970
Why?

00:52:01.970 --> 00:52:06.383
Because they've operated there on the
principal, no vanquished,

00:52:06.383 --> 00:52:09.360
no victor, you get your milkshake I get
mine.

00:52:12.070 --> 00:52:14.370
Big lesson I got early in my career in
journalism,

00:52:14.370 --> 00:52:17.310
never ask the competition to hold the
phone for you.

00:52:20.830 --> 00:52:23.030
This is the first lesson I ever learned as
a journalist.

00:52:23.030 --> 00:52:24.920
When I was a cub reporter just starting

00:52:24.920 --> 00:52:27.163
out in London, the first real news story
that

00:52:27.163 --> 00:52:28.757
my editors at UPI sent me to was in

00:52:28.757 --> 00:52:31.940
the Iran, covered the Iranian embassy
takeover in London.

00:52:31.940 --> 00:52:34.090
It involved pro-Khomeini Iranian students.

00:52:34.090 --> 00:52:35.600
Somehow I managed to talk my way into

00:52:35.600 --> 00:52:37.370
the building, interviewed some of the
student rebels.

00:52:37.370 --> 00:52:40.610
I don't remember what they said but I was
so excited by whatever

00:52:40.610 --> 00:52:43.560
it was that I ran right to the phone booth
next to the embassy.

00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:45.760
Big red old British style phone booth.

00:52:45.760 --> 00:52:46.770
No cell phones back then.

00:52:46.770 --> 00:52:49.030
It was one of these classics.

00:52:49.030 --> 00:52:50.020
There was a long line.

00:52:50.020 --> 00:52:50.790
I got in line.

00:52:50.790 --> 00:52:52.260
All these other grizzled Fleet Street

00:52:52.260 --> 00:52:53.960
reporters who wanted to file their
stories.

00:52:53.960 --> 00:52:56.200
I patiently waited to my turn at the
phone.

00:52:56.200 --> 00:52:58.960
When I got inside the booth, I excitedly
called my editors at UPI.

00:52:58.960 --> 00:53:03.030
I, telling them everything I had seen and
heard from inside the embassy.

00:53:03.030 --> 00:53:04.570
Reading from pages in my notebook.

00:53:04.570 --> 00:53:08.500
At one point though, one of the editors
said to me, taking down my dictation.

00:53:08.500 --> 00:53:11.600
Can you check this detail in the Embassy?

00:53:11.600 --> 00:53:13.310
I said, wait a minute, I'll check.

00:53:13.310 --> 00:53:16.089
I opened the door of that little red phone
booth, and said to the

00:53:16.089 --> 00:53:20.490
fleet step reporter standing in line
behind me, do me a favor, hold the phone.

00:53:20.490 --> 00:53:22.470
Then, I dashed out of the phone booth.

00:53:22.470 --> 00:53:24.107
Before I had taken two steps, the guy in

00:53:24.107 --> 00:53:26.293
line behind me slipped into the booth,
slammed down

00:53:26.293 --> 00:53:28.926
the receiver, started dialing his own
newspaper, and turned

00:53:28.926 --> 00:53:31.310
to me to say two words, I will never
forget.

00:53:31.310 --> 00:53:31.970
Sorry mate.

00:53:33.410 --> 00:53:36.160
Never ask the competition to hold the
phone for you.

00:53:37.680 --> 00:53:43.400
Now I will conclude on a very personal
note for all you business students,

00:53:43.400 --> 00:53:50.460
always be a big tipper, always be nice to
the help, and always call your mother.

00:53:50.460 --> 00:53:53.400
Okay, always be a big tipper.

00:53:53.400 --> 00:53:55.550
I learned this from my friend the film
maker George Stevens.

00:53:55.550 --> 00:53:59.910
You know if you are a big tipper it may
actually add up to $5,000.

00:53:59.910 --> 00:54:01.310
Yeah, up to $5,000.

00:54:01.310 --> 00:54:05.030
If your a big tipper every day every meal
your whole life.

00:54:05.030 --> 00:54:07.760
But you'll make a difference in someone
else's life.

00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:11.070
You'll actually be giving them a sign of
respect.

00:54:11.070 --> 00:54:13.490
Always be a big tipper.

00:54:13.490 --> 00:54:15.750
Always, always be nice to the help.

00:54:15.750 --> 00:54:17.590
It's not only the right thing to do but I
can tell

00:54:17.590 --> 00:54:21.310
you as a reporter, the secretaries who
have been putting me through

00:54:21.310 --> 00:54:25.560
to their boss, the policeman who have let
me through a barricade

00:54:25.560 --> 00:54:30.738
because I was nice to them when it didn't
count really came back.

00:54:30.738 --> 00:54:34.440
To serve me later.

00:54:34.440 --> 00:54:37.630
Always be nice to the help, and lastly

00:54:37.630 --> 00:54:40.380
this is advice I always give at every
graduation.

00:54:40.380 --> 00:54:43.010
Always call your mother.

00:54:43.010 --> 00:54:45.450
Where's my daughter?

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:46.340
Always call your mother.

00:54:48.380 --> 00:54:51.790
Whenever I've had the honor of giving a
college graduation speech, I always end

00:54:51.790 --> 00:54:53.400
it with this story about the legendary

00:54:53.400 --> 00:54:56.110
University of Alabama football coach Bear
Bryant.

00:54:56.110 --> 00:54:58.700
Late in his career after his mother had
died, South Central

00:54:58.700 --> 00:55:02.120
Bell Telephone Company asked Bear Bryant
to do a TV commercial.

00:55:02.120 --> 00:55:05.080
As best as I can piece together, the
commercial was supposed to be very simple.

00:55:05.080 --> 00:55:06.460
Just a little music.

00:55:06.460 --> 00:55:08.470
And Coach Bear Bryant saying in his tough

00:55:08.470 --> 00:55:12.550
gravely voice, have you called your mama
today?

00:55:12.550 --> 00:55:14.900
On the day of the filming though, he
decided to ad lib something.

00:55:14.900 --> 00:55:18.790
He reportedly looked into the camera and
said, have you called your mama today?

00:55:18.790 --> 00:55:20.580
I sure wish I could call mine.

00:55:20.580 --> 00:55:21.080
His

00:55:22.750 --> 00:55:25.810
mother had just died, and that was how the
commercial ran.

00:55:25.810 --> 00:55:27.750
And it got a huge response from audiences.

00:55:29.080 --> 00:55:33.070
So if you take anything away from this
lecture, please take this.

00:55:34.170 --> 00:55:37.740
Call your mother always, and your father.

00:55:38.750 --> 00:55:39.710
I wish I could call mine.

00:55:41.330 --> 00:55:43.240
So I'm fresh out of ammo here folks.

00:55:43.240 --> 00:55:45.090
Let me just conclude by saying this.

00:55:46.780 --> 00:55:50.240
That's why I want to write this book on a
column, right?

00:55:50.240 --> 00:55:52.410
This is where my opinions come from.

00:55:52.410 --> 00:55:54.180
And that's what constitutes a real column.

00:55:55.530 --> 00:55:57.030
And these are some of the things that I've

00:55:57.030 --> 00:56:00.000
learned writing one for 20 years twice a
week.

00:56:01.370 --> 00:56:03.280
It's still the best job in the world.

00:56:03.280 --> 00:56:03.920
I have to tell you.

00:56:03.920 --> 00:56:05.400
I've got the best job in the world.

00:56:05.400 --> 00:56:06.500
And somebody has to have it.

00:56:06.500 --> 00:56:08.490
I've got it and you don't.

00:56:08.490 --> 00:56:08.990
Okay?

00:56:11.480 --> 00:56:14.054
I get to be a tourist with an attitude, go
where

00:56:14.054 --> 00:56:17.429
ever I want, write whatever I want, about
whatever I want.

00:56:17.429 --> 00:56:20.819
Some 45 years after taking journalism in
that room at 313

00:56:20.819 --> 00:56:24.469
room 313 at Sandal's Park High school, I
can honestly tell you

00:56:24.469 --> 00:56:27.336
that I'm as excited about writing my next
column as I

00:56:27.336 --> 00:56:31.530
was my first and I am also Les still
looking for Minnesota.

00:56:31.530 --> 00:56:34.860
That's why I'm gonna end where I begin,
with the Jersey Boys.

00:56:34.860 --> 00:56:36.990
Because the closing lines of that musical
play

00:56:36.990 --> 00:56:39.330
resonate with me just like the opening
ones.

00:56:39.330 --> 00:56:42.200
This time it's Frankie Valli talking,
looking back on his

00:56:42.200 --> 00:56:45.070
remarkable career as the lead singer of
the Four Seasons.

00:56:46.260 --> 00:56:49.330
They ask, he says, What was the highpoint?

00:56:49.330 --> 00:56:50.600
The Hall of Fame?

00:56:50.600 --> 00:56:52.240
Selling all those records?

00:56:52.240 --> 00:56:54.010
Pulling Sheri out of the hat.

00:56:54.010 --> 00:56:55.320
It was all great.

00:56:55.320 --> 00:56:58.880
But the first time the four of us made
that sound, our sound, when

00:56:58.880 --> 00:57:03.610
everything dropped away and all there was
was the music, that was the best.

00:57:03.610 --> 00:57:08.460
That's why I'm still out there singing
like that bunny on TV with the battery.

00:57:08.460 --> 00:57:11.530
I just keep going and going and going.

00:57:11.530 --> 00:57:13.240
Chasing the music...

00:57:13.240 --> 00:57:13.740
Trying

00:57:15.840 --> 00:57:19.300
to get home.

00:57:21.360 --> 00:57:23.075
Thank you very much.

00:57:23.075 --> 00:57:25.324
[SOUND]

